<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Plain HTTP to the property network.

  This is the setting that decides whether the app works at all. Android 9 and later
  refuse cleartext HTTP by default, and the host serves `http://192.168.x.x:4700` because
  it is an offline LAN with no certificate authority to get one from. Without this file
  every request fails with a cleartext-not-permitted error that looks, from the phone,
  exactly like the host being switched off.

  Why this is not scoped to the private ranges: Android's <domain> element matches host
  names literally (plus subdomains). It does not understand CIDR, so an entry like
  `192.168.0.0/16` never matches `192.168.1.50` and cleartext stays blocked. There is no
  way to express "any RFC1918 address" here, and the host's address is not known at build
  time. The app only ever talks to the base URL it was paired with, so allowing cleartext
  generally does not widen what it actually reaches.

  If the host is later given a locally-trusted certificate, add a <trust-anchors> block
  pointing at the CA and set cleartextTrafficPermitted to false — the app will then refuse
  to fall back to plaintext, which is the right end state.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
